<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Nicholson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1728">1728</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Nicholson was an English army officer and colonial administrator who governed several American colonies. As lieutenant governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Sir Edmund Andros</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, he was driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leislers-rebellion">Leisler's Rebellion</a> in 1689. He later governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, where he moved the capital to Williamsburg, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francis Nicholson was born at Downholme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> on 12 November 1655. Little is known of his family. He served as a page to Charles Paulet, later Duke of Bolton, whose patronage advanced his career, and in 1678 Paulet bought him an ensign's commission in the Holland Regiment. In 1680 he joined the 2nd Tangier Regiment, sent to reinforce English Tangier, where he carried dispatches between the Moroccan camp, Tangier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and came to the notice of the colonial secretary William Blathwayt. In 1686 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> as a captain with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Sir Edmund Andros</a>, who sent him to observe the defences of Port Royal in French Acadia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As lieutenant governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> from 1688 he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with a council but no assembly, and told colonists they were a conquered people. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> revolt of 1689 he kept the news quiet. A quarrel on 30 May, in which the hot-tempered Nicholson said he would rather see the town on fire than be commanded by a militia officer, set off the rising: the militia took Fort James, Jacob Leisler took command, and on 24 June Nicholson sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>As lieutenant governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> until 1692 he helped create the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William and Mary</a> and was one of its first trustees. Losing the governorship of Virginia to Andros deepened a feud between them; he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, then Virginia from 1698 to 1705, and laid out Annapolis and Williamsburg. He proposed uniting the colonies under one viceroy with powers of taxation and a standing army, one of the first such plans, according to the historian John Fiske. In Queen Anne's War he captured Port Royal on 2 October 1710, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> from 1712 to 1715 and became the first royal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> in 1721.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nicholson rose to lieutenant-general and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> unmarried; he was buried in the parish of St George Hanover Square. His temper was notorious, and the historian George Waller wrote that he was subject to fits of passion. His courtship of the young Lucy Burwell, who refused him, contributed to a petition by six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> councillors to Queen Anne for his removal. He was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> and the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts. Nicholson Hall at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William and Mary</a> and Francis and Nicholson streets in Colonial Williamsburg bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis Nicholson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andros governed New York through his lieutenant <b>Francis Nicholson</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-new-england">Dominion of New England</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Downholme, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.38,-1.83&amp;year=1702" aria-label="Downholme on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4510 300 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis Nicholson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4960 570h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4960 570h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.38,-1.83&amp;year=1702">What was here in 1702? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
